fix(backup): retry a transient failure and say what is at risk (#267)

A backup that could not reach the ledger slept a full interval, so a database
restart of about two minutes cost a day of unprotected work, and nothing
escalated the unhealthy container.

backup-ledger.sh now separates the two failures. Exit 6 is a ledger that could
not be reached: no dump was attempted, so nothing has been learned about any
dump. Exit 5 is a dump that was produced and is not usable, and exit 3 remains
a dump that disagrees with a sealed segment. A mid-run failure is classified by
asking the ledger again, never by reading the client's error text. The
verification step is unchanged: every dump is still restored into a throwaway
database and adjudicated against the sealed segments before it is called good.

backup-loop.sh retries only the transient class, from 60s doubling to 900s and
resetting on success; a mismatch or an unusable dump waits the full interval
and says why. SIGUSR1 forces a run, which replaces deleting the marker the
healthcheck reads. The marker vocabulary is untouched, so a transient failure
still reads as unhealthy rather than falling past backup-health.sh's case.

Every failing run measures what is exposed -- how long since the last dump that
actually verified, and how many events lie above it -- prints it, and leaves it
in .exposure for the loop to repeat. It is cleared at the start of every run and
by every run that succeeds.

Driven by BAK-005..008 in backup-ledger-selftest.sh and BAK-009..013 in
backup-service-selftest.sh, all watched failing first.

